from science_completion import q,p,m
QUESTIONS=[
q(1,'What is potable water?','Water safe to drink.','4.10.1.2'),q(1,'Name a method that removes dissolved salt from seawater.','Distillation.','4.10.1.2'),q(1,'What is the solid formed by sewage sedimentation called?','Sludge.','4.10.1.3'),q(1,'Which organisms carry out bioleaching?','Bacteria.','4.10.1.4','2023 2H Q05.4'),q(1,'What two substances must be present for iron to rust?','Oxygen and water.','4.10.3.1'),q(1,'Which metal commonly galvanises iron?','Zinc.','4.10.3.1'),q(1,'Which two metals form bronze?','Copper and tin.','4.10.3.2'),q(1,'Which two metals form brass?','Copper and zinc.','4.10.3.2'),q(1,'What percentage gold is in 18-carat gold?','75%.','4.10.3.2'),q(1,'What catalyst is used in the Haber process?','Iron.','4.10.4.1'),q(1,'What does NPK stand for?','Nitrogen, phosphorus and potassium.','4.10.4.2'),q(1,'Which polymer type has covalent cross-links and does not melt on heating?','Thermosetting.','4.10.3.3'),
q(2,'Distinguish renewable from finite resources.','- Renewable resources can be replenished on useful timescales if managed appropriately.\n- Finite resources are used far faster than they are replaced, so available reserves decline.','4.10.1.1'),
q(2,'Define sustainable development.','- Development meeting **current generations’ needs**.\n- Without compromising **future generations’ ability to meet theirs**.','4.10.1.1'),
q(2,'Why is potable water not necessarily chemically pure?','- It is safe because microbes and harmful substances are sufficiently low.\n- It still contains **dissolved substances**, so it is not only H₂O.','4.10.1.2'),
q(2,'Give one purpose of filtering water and one purpose of sterilising it.','- Filtering removes **suspended solid particles**.\n- Sterilising kills microorganisms.','4.10.1.2'),
q(2,'Give two sterilisation methods for freshwater supplies.','Chlorine and ultraviolet light.','4.10.1.2'),
q(2,'Why does filtering seawater not usually make it potable?','- Ordinary filters do not remove the **dissolved salts**.\n- Desalination, such as distillation or reverse osmosis, is needed to reduce salt levels sufficiently.','4.10.1.2'),
q(2,'Describe how distillation removes dissolved salts.','- Water evaporates, leaving dissolved salts behind.\n- Vapour is cooled and **condensed** into collected water.','4.10.1.2'),
q(2,'Distinguish sewage-sludge treatment from effluent treatment.','- Sludge undergoes **anaerobic digestion**.\n- Effluent undergoes **aerobic biological treatment**.','4.10.1.3'),
q(2,'Describe bioleaching of copper compounds.','- Bacteria help produce a **leachate solution**.\n- The leachate contains dissolved copper compounds for further metal recovery.','4.10.1.4','2023 2H Q05.4'),
q(2,'Why can scrap iron recover copper from copper-salt solution?','- Iron is **more reactive** than copper.\n- It displaces copper from its compound, producing copper metal.','4.10.1.4'),
q(2,'Give two reasons recycling copper can be better than extracting more ore.','- It conserves **finite ore resources**.\n- It can require less energy and cause less mining damage than fresh extraction.','4.10.2.2','2022 2H Q01.4: original wording'),
q(2,'Why can painting fail to protect scratched iron?','- A scratch breaks the **barrier**.\n- Water and oxygen can then reach the iron and cause rusting.','4.10.3.1'),
q(2,'Explain why zinc can protect scratched galvanised iron.','- Zinc is more reactive and **oxidises preferentially**.\n- It gives **sacrificial protection** while enough connected zinc remains.','4.10.3.1'),
q(2,'Why does aluminium resist further corrosion after its surface reacts?','- It forms an adherent **oxide coating**.\n- The coating acts as a barrier to further reaction of the underlying metal.','4.10.3.1'),
q(2,'State a use of stainless steel and a property supporting it.','- Cutlery or kitchen equipment.\n- It is hard and **corrosion-resistant**.','4.10.3.2'),
q(2,'State a use of low-carbon steel and a supporting property.','- Car body panels.\n- It is comparatively soft and **easily shaped**.','4.10.3.2'),
q(2,'Give a use of high-carbon steel and its relevant limitation.','- Cutting tools need its hardness/strength.\n- It is **brittle**, which can limit uses requiring bending or impact resistance.','4.10.3.2'),
q(2,'Explain why aluminium alloys suit aircraft structures.','- They have **low density**, reducing mass.\n- Suitable alloys combine this with useful strength.','4.10.3.2'),
q(2,'Name the three main starting materials for soda-lime glass.','Sand, sodium carbonate and limestone.','4.10.3.3'),
q(2,'What two roles make up a composite material?','- A **matrix/binder** holds the structure together.\n- A **reinforcement**, such as fibres, supplies useful strength or other properties.','4.10.3.3'),
q(2,'State a usual source of each Haber-process reactant.','- Nitrogen from **air**.\n- Hydrogen from **natural gas**.','4.10.4.1','2022 2H Q07.1'),
q(2,'What happens to ammonia and unused gases after the Haber reactor?','- Ammonia is cooled so it **liquefies and is removed**.\n- Unreacted nitrogen/hydrogen are **recycled**.','4.10.4.1','2022 2H Q07.3'),
q(2,'Why is untreated phosphate rock not an effective direct fertiliser?','- It is **poorly soluble**.\n- Nutrients cannot readily dissolve and become available for plant uptake.','4.10.4.2','2023 2H Q10.5'),
q(2,'Name the fertiliser products associated with sulfuric and phosphoric acid treatment of phosphate rock.','- Sulfuric acid: **single superphosphate**, including calcium sulfate and soluble phosphate compounds.\n- Phosphoric acid: **triple superphosphate**, mainly calcium dihydrogenphosphate.','4.10.4.2','2023 2H Q10.6'),
q(3,'Describe phytomining and the form of the recovered material.','- Plants absorb metal compounds from soil.\n- Harvest and burn the biomass.\n- Ash contains **metal compounds**, requiring further processing to obtain metal.','4.10.1.4'),
q(3,'An empty basin is 24.50 g; after evaporating 50 cm³ water it is 24.65 g. Calculate residue mass and concentration in g dm⁻³.','- Residue = 24.65−24.50 = **0.15 g**.\n- Volume = 50/1000 = **0.050 dm³**.\n- Concentration = 0.15/0.050 = **3.0 g dm⁻³**.','4.10.1.2;RPA8'),
q(3,'A 16 g jewellery alloy is 18 carat. Calculate gold mass and explain why alloying is useful.','- Gold fraction = 18/24 = **0.75**.\n- Gold mass = 16 ×0.75 = **12 g**.\n- Alloying increases hardness/wear resistance compared with soft pure gold.','4.10.3.2'),
q(3,'Explain why alloys are usually harder than the pure metal.','- Pure metal has relatively regular layers of similar-sized atoms.\n- Other elements introduce different sizes and **distort the layers**.\n- Layers slide less easily.','4.10.3.2'),
q(3,'Describe manufacturing clay ceramics.','- Shape **wet clay** into the required form.\n- Dry as appropriate and heat strongly in a **furnace**.\n- The resulting hard material can form pottery or bricks, though it is brittle.','4.10.3.3'),
q(3,'Explain how LDPE and HDPE can both be made from ethene yet have different properties.','- Different conditions/catalysts produce different chain arrangements.\n- LDPE’s greater branching prevents close packing.\n- HDPE packs more closely, giving higher density and greater rigidity.','4.10.3.3'),
q(3,'Explain why thermosetting polymers do not melt like thermosoftening polymers.','- Thermosetting chains have **covalent cross-links**.\n- These prevent chains moving freely on heating.\n- Thermosoftening chains are separate, so overcoming intermolecular attractions allows movement and melting.','4.10.3.3'),
q(3,'State the Haber equation and explain the gas ratio.','- **N₂+3H₂ ⇌2NH₃**.\n- One nitrogen molecule reacts with three hydrogen molecules.\n- Feed gases therefore use a **1:3 nitrogen:hydrogen molar ratio**.','4.10.4.1'),
q(3,'Explain why high pressure favours ammonia in the Haber process.','- There are **four gas moles** on the reactant side and **two** on the product side.\n- High pressure favours fewer gas moles.\n- Equilibrium shifts towards ammonia, increasing its proportion.','4.10.4.1'),
q(3,'Name the products from nitric acid and phosphate rock, and link one to fertiliser use.','- Products include **calcium nitrate**.\n- **Phosphoric acid** is also produced.\n- Calcium nitrate supplies plant nutrients in a soluble salt; phosphoric acid can support further phosphate-fertiliser manufacture.','4.10.4.2'),
q(4,'Describe a controlled experiment showing rust needs both oxygen and water.','- Use comparable clean iron nails, same time and temperature.\n- One nail has air and water and should rust.\n- One has dry air with a drying agent, preventing water exposure.\n- One has boiled water covered with oil, removing/excluding oxygen; little/no rust in the latter two supports both requirements.','4.10.3.1'),
q(4,'Explain the four major life-cycle stages and why transport matters.','- Raw-material extraction/processing.\n- Manufacture and packaging.\n- Use over the product’s useful lifetime.\n- Disposal/reuse/recycling, with **transport between stages** adding energy use and emissions.','4.10.2.1'),
q(4,'Explain why a reusable glass bottle can have lower impact per use despite higher production energy.','- Manufacture impact is spread over many uses.\n- Reuse avoids making a new bottle for every use.\n- Washing and return transport still consume resources/energy.\n- Actual reuse count, breakage and travel determine whether the overall comparison is favourable.','4.10.2.1','2022 2H Q03.1: adapted LCA interpretation'),
q(4,'Describe the main wastewater treatment sequence.','- Screen/remove grit.\n- Sediment to separate **sludge and effluent**.\n- Digest sludge **anaerobically**.\n- Treat effluent **aerobically**, with extra chemical treatment if required by contaminants.','4.10.1.3'),
q(6,'Evaluate a biological method for extracting copper from low-grade material.','- Phytomining concentrates compounds in plant ash; bioleaching produces a solution of metal compounds.\n- Copper still requires recovery, for example iron displacement or electrolysis.\n- These methods can reduce excavation of huge rock volumes and use deposits unsuitable for conventional extraction.\n- They may be slow, require land/controlled conditions and risk harmful leachate.\n- Compare measured yield, time, energy, cost and pollution with mining/recycling alternatives.\n- A justified choice depends on the deposit and controls, rather than biological methods being automatically harmless.','4.10.1.4'),
q(6,'Evaluate paper and plastic shopping bags using a fair LCA.','- Compare the same load-carrying service and realistic reuse numbers.\n- Include raw materials, manufacture, transport, use and disposal/recycling.\n- Paper may use renewable feedstock but considerable water/energy; plastic can be light but often uses finite feedstock.\n- Durability, litter, disposal and collection affect the practical outcome.\n- Use reliable quantitative energy/resource data while recognising value judgements in weighting impacts.\n- Give a conclusion tied to the evidence and assumptions, avoiding a selective single-stage advertisement.','4.10.2.1'),
q(6,'Explain why the Haber process uses about 450°C, 200 atmospheres and an iron catalyst.','- Forward ammonia formation is exothermic, so lower temperature favours equilibrium yield.\n- Very low temperature gives too slow a rate; about 450°C balances yield and useful production speed.\n- Pressure favours ammonia because four reactant gas moles become two product gas moles.\n- Excessive pressure costs compression energy and stronger safe equipment; around 200 atmospheres is a practical compromise.\n- Iron lowers activation energy and speeds both directions without changing equilibrium yield.\n- Cooling removes liquid ammonia and unused gases are recycled, improving overall production.','4.10.4.1','2022 2H Q07.6'),
q(6,'Compare preparing an ammonium fertiliser salt in a laboratory and in an integrated industrial process.','- Both use appropriate chemical reactions and controlled quantities, such as ammonia neutralisation with acid.\n- Laboratory titration can determine proportions, followed by an indicator-free mixture and crystallisation.\n- Industrial manufacture uses larger-scale equipment, often continuous flow, monitoring and heat management.\n- Raw-material and acid production can be integrated, with useful streams recycled.\n- Final NPK composition must meet plant nutrient needs and purity requirements.\n- Evaluate supplied yield, energy, safety and cost data rather than assuming classroom apparatus simply scales up unchanged.','4.10.4.2')]
